+# lab/bio-2026-11: mechanisms taken from biology
+
+From f430207. Host: the WSL2 guest of the Ryzen host (12 threads, Go 1.24.7), no
+performance counters used here: ns/op read by interleaved rounds is indicative
+only; the deterministic readings (answers, counters, lines touched) are the
+ones a verdict rests on. Each arm's rule is committed before its reading.
+
+## B1 Scent marks on full lines
+
+**The analogy.** A foraging bumblebee leaves a short-lived scent mark on a
+flower it has emptied; other bees skip marked flowers, and the mark fades at
+about the rate nectar refills. A cuckoo search is a forager: it loads the
+alternate line of each candidate entry to learn, most of the time (a line is
+full with probability ~0.66 at 85% load), that it has no room.
+
+**The mechanism.** One byte per line: "these four slots are live until unit
+U". Whoever scans a line and finds four live entries (an insert's own two
+lines, a search's leaf scan) writes the mark from the oldest last touch it
+saw. A search consults the mark before it loads a leaf line and skips the
+load while the mark holds; the first-level prefetch skips marked lines too
+(they are loaded only if the search has to go deeper). The mark fades by
+arithmetic, as slots do: a touch only extends a slot's life, so the mark stays
+a lower bound. An install lowers a line's mark when the entry going in expires
+earlier (a relocated copy keeps its old last touch); `abandon` and the sweep
+clear it. Marks older than 200 units are bulk-cleared, so a byte's unit
+(mod 255) can't alias.
+
+A marked leaf still spends search budget, so the search visits the same
+entries in the same order and decides the same: single-threaded, every answer
+and counter must be identical. Under concurrency a mark can be stale for at
+most a horizon (a scan racing an `abandon`); the cost is a usable line
+skipped, never an answer: no guarantee depends on a search finding room.
+
+Marks are consulted by searches only: hits, counts and absent-key reads never
+read them (E41's lesson). `TF_MARKS=0` leaves them out (the base arm, in the
+same binary, so both arms share one code layout).
+
+**Rule (before any reading).**
+
+1. Identity: `tfbench -exact` byte-identical to `verify/expected/exact.txt`
+   in both arms; `TestConformance` passes; `go test -short` passes plain,
+   `TF_BG=1`, `TF_CT=1`; `go test -race -short` passes.
+2. Work: on tfbench's churn stream, the distinct alternate lines a search
+   touches (prefetched, scanned or expanded), per search, fall at least 2x at
+   load 1.0 (85% slot load). Read also at 1.1 and 1.15.
+3. Speed, indicative: `InsertSteady` and `CheckAndTouchMissInsert` at most
+   0.95 of the base (median of 8 interleaved rounds); `CheckAndTouchHit`,
+   `TouchAndCount`, `ContainsAbsent` and `InsertSaturated` at most 1.03.
+4. Memory: reported as read (`Bytes()` per key at design load).
+
+A candidate if 1 and 2 hold and 3 holds; otherwise recorded as read. Not
+adopted here in any case: it costs every filter a byte per line, which is the
+owner's decision, and its concurrent behaviour has no model.
+
+## B3 A fovea for hot entries (a model)
+
+**The analogy.** A retina spends its acuity where the gaze is: cones are
+packed in the fovea, and the eye moves the fovea onto what matters. A hot
+entry is where the filter already looks hardest: the heavy keys, the ones a
+rate limit exists for. Promotion gives such a key wider counters (range) but
+the same 2.5 s unit (acuity), so `Upper` on a window shorter than a few units
+still counts a whole unit bucket at the window's edge, and `TouchIfUnder`
+admits 30–65% of a limit on 1–5 s windows (paper §4.2).
+
+**The mechanism.** A hot entry keeps its newest 4 units also as 32 sub-unit
+buckets (unit/8, 312.5 ms at a 60 s horizon; 16-bit counts, 64 B: a second
+line per hot entry). `Upper` for a window whose edge falls inside those units
+counts the sub-bucket the edge cuts instead of the unit bucket. Older buckets
+and every other key are unchanged.
+
+**The model** (`TestLabFovea`): one hot key offered Poisson traffic for 10
+horizons behind three strict limiters (admit while `Upper` < limit): an exact
+sliding log, the hot entry as it is, and the foveal entry. Windows 1, 2.5, 5,
+10 and 15 s, limits of 20–100 events/s, offered 0.8x, 1.2x and 2x the limit.
+
+**Rule (before any reading).** Worth building if, at the 1 s and 5 s windows:
+
+1. offered 2x the limit, the foveal arm admits at least 0.75 of what the
+   exact log admits;
+2. offered 0.8x the limit, its false denies (denied while its own admitted
+   events were under the limit) are at most half the base arm's;
+3. no arm admits past the limit in any window (asserted).
+
+The base arm is read alongside: if it does not land near the paper's 30–65%
+the model is not the filter and the reading is void. A model's verdict is
+"worth building" at most: the entry's protocol (HotEntry.tla) and the memory
+(0.6 B per key more once a hot table exists, or half the entries) are not
+read here.
+
+## B1b, B1c Scent marks: wider marking, and marked leaves that cost no budget
+
+Designed after B1's work reading (1.62x at load 1.0, under its 2x; 29% of
+lines marked at the end where about half are full), so these rules are
+written knowing it.
+
+**B1b** (`TF_MARKS=2`). An insert that finds its primary line full and lands
+in its alternate line marks the primary line too (B1 marked an insert's own
+lines only when both were full). Same decisions as the base, so:
+
+- answers and counters identical to the base on the three churn streams;
+- lines per search at load 1.0 at least 2x fewer than the base.
+
+**B1c** (`TF_MARKS=3`). B1b, and a marked leaf spends no search budget: the
+budget caps lines loaded, and a marked leaf loads none. This changes which
+searches succeed, so placements differ from the base and from the
+conformance vectors: a format-level change, read here only for its size.
+
+- missed 0 and false_seen 0 on the three churn streams;
+- evictions at load 1.15 at least 25% under the base's 4,577;
+- lines per search no higher than B1b's at any load.
+
+## Readings
+
+### B1 scent marks: not a candidate (clauses 2 and 3 fail)
+
+1. Identity: holds but for the memory it costs. `tfbench -exact` differs
+   from `verify/expected/exact.txt` in one line, `memory ... 19.1 B/key`
+   (18.8); every answer and counter is the same, and `TF_MARKS=0` is
+   byte-identical. `TestConformance` and `TestRegress` pass. `go test -short`
+   passes with `TF_BG=1`, `TF_CT=1` and `TF_MARKS=0`; plain, and under
+   `-race` (0 races, 448 s), one test fails, `TestMemoryOnFirstUse` (78,328
+   bytes at 85% load, want the table's 77,120): the byte per line again.
+2. Work (`TF_LAB=1 go test -run TestLabMarksWork -v`): distinct alternate
+   lines per search, base → marks:
+
+   | churn load | searches (of unseen touches) | lines per search | fewer | lines marked at the end |
+   |---|---|---|---|---|
+   | 1.00 | 17.5% | 8.21 → 5.07 | 1.62x | 29.0% |
+   | 1.10 | 32.5% | 11.10 → 5.44 | 2.04x | 52.2% |
+   | 1.15 | 44.4% | 64.88 → 21.25 | 3.05x | 70.5% |
+
+   Under the 2x the rule asked at load 1.00. Evictions (0, 0, 4,577),
+   relocations, missed and false seen identical in both arms.
+3. Speed (3 interleaved rounds, not the 8 of the rule: every pair agreed in
+   direction and the spread was under 2%; ns/op, marks/base by medians):
+   InsertSteady 481 → 578 (1.20), CheckAndTouchMissInsert 1,935 → 2,093
+   (1.08), InsertSaturated 1,400 → 1,597 (1.14), a 4M-key InsertSteady past
+   the cache 539 → 718 (1.33), InsertNearFull 6,082 → 5,720 (0.94); Hit
+   1.014, TouchAndCount 1.001, ContainsAbsent 1.001. Slower where the rule
+   asked 0.95.
+4. Memory: 18.8 → 19.1 B per key at design load.
+
+**Why it is slower with fewer lines.** A line load is not the unit of cost.
+The base issues the eight first-level loads unconditionally, so their misses
+overlap: one or two miss rounds, whatever the count. The mark check puts a
+load of its own (a byte in a 294 KB array, 1.2 MB at 4M keys) and a branch
+the predictor can't call (a mark is set on 30–70% of lines) ahead of every
+line load, so the loads that remain no longer overlap. A profile of
+InsertSteady puts `warmLine` at 2.05 s of 7.19 s in the base and 2.74 s of
+8.12 s with marks, `markByte` and `markAhead` at 0.96 s more. It is E2's
+lesson (branchless-find) the other way round. The marks pay only where a
+search goes deep enough that lines, not rounds, bound it (load 1.15).
+
+### B1b: fails (1.71x at load 1.00, under 2x)
+
+Lines per search 4.80, 5.35, 21.20 at the three loads (1.71x, 2.07x, 3.06x);
+31.5% of lines marked at the end of load 1.00 against B1's 29.0%. Answers and
+counters identical to the base. What limits the marks is not who writes them
+but that a line is learned full only when someone scans it.
+
+### B1c: fails as written (clause 3); the eviction reading holds
+
+Missed 0, false seen 0 on the three streams. Evictions at load 1.15: 4,577 →
+1,238 (−73%), relocations 932,499 → 1,000,045, for 26.96 lines per search:
+2.41x fewer than the base's 64.88 but more than B1b's 21.20, which clause 3
+barred (a rule that asked a deeper search to load no more than a shallower
+one). E31's breadth-first search at the same budget reached 2,703 evictions
+and at twice the budget 369, slower both times; this arm is under the base
+on both counts in lines. In time it is not: InsertNearFull 6,082 → 7,941 ns
+(1.31), 0.0208 → 0.0085 evictions an insert, and with every leaf marked a
+saturated table's search never spends its budget: InsertSaturated 2.08,
+MissInsert 1.85. It would need a cap of its own on marked leaves.
+
+### B3 a fovea for hot entries: passes its rule (a model): worth building
+
+`TF_LAB=1 go test -run TestLabFovea -v`; admitted as a share of what an
+exact sliding log admits, and requests denied while the arm's own admitted
+events were under the limit, as a share of offered:
+
+| window | limit | offered | admitted: base | fovea | false denies: base | fovea |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 s | 20 | 0.8x | 0.523 | 0.909 | 0.510 | 0.147 |
+| 1 s | 20 | 2x | 0.429 | 0.833 | 0.699 | 0.561 |
+| 1 s | 100 | 0.8x | 0.502 | 0.921 | 0.501 | 0.083 |
+| 1 s | 100 | 2x | 0.406 | 0.808 | 0.703 | 0.573 |
+| 2.5 s | 100 | 0.8x | 0.632 | 0.988 | 0.370 | 0.015 |
+| 5 s | 100 | 0.8x | 0.644 | 0.996 | 0.358 | 0.007 |
+| 5 s | 100 | 2x | 0.510 | 0.951 | 0.743 | 0.413 |
+| 5 s | 500 | 0.8x | 0.627 | 1.000 | 0.373 | 0.000 |
+| 5 s | 500 | 2x | 0.501 | 0.943 | 0.746 | 0.189 |
+| 10 s | 1000 | 0.8x | 0.625 | 0.626 | 0.375 | 0.374 |
+| 15 s | 300 | 0.8x | 0.704 | 0.704 | 0.296 | 0.296 |
+
+(The 1.2x rows are in the test's output.) The base arm reads 0.41–0.64 on
+1–5 s windows, inside the paper's 30–65%, so the model is the filter's
+`Upper`. Clause 1: 0.81–0.83 at 1 s and 0.94–0.95 at 5 s, over 0.75. Clause
+2: false denies 3.5–6x under the base at 1 s, 48x and more at 5 s. Clause 3
+asserted, never tripped. Windows of 10 s and more reach past the four
+foveated units and read as the base, as designed.
+
+Limits of the reading. A model of one key: no entry protocol (the sub-bucket
+words would join `HotEntry.tla`'s writers), no memory reading (a second line
+per hot entry: 0.6 B per key more once a hot table exists, or half as many
+entries in the same bytes), no `Count`. And the limit-20 rows can't happen
+by today's promotion: a key held to 20 a second by `TouchIfUnder` is
+admitted about 20 times a unit, under the 31 that promote it, so it stays on
+its register. Those rows need promotion on an uncertain denial (`Upper` at
+the limit, `Count` under it) rather than on overflow: the next arm, with the
+number of entries that takes as its reading.
